   “You went,” said Manny.
   “Nineteen twenty-six.”
   “Were they good?”
   “Half of them.” I took my hand off the column. “Which half is the
question.”



Vale arrived at seven.
   He came in through the main door in a dark suit, tall and gray, the
good coat over his arm, trailed by a small cloud of donors who had
clearly been told he was a remarkable man and were now deciding
whether it was true. He shook hands as he walked. He stopped to say
something to a woman at the champagne tower that made her laugh.
He moved through the hall like a man who’d been in this room a hun‐
dred times, because, I thought, he had. His father had walked it. His
grandfather. A Vale had counted something in this armory since before
any of us were born.
   He saw me from fifty feet away. He smiled.
   He took his time coming over. When he arrived, he stopped exactly
two steps from me, so as not to loom, and let a pause open.
   I counted.
   “Neighbor,” said Vale. “You came.”
   “It was a good pass.”
   “I hoped you’d think so.” His eyes went to the case on the trolley,
with its gaffer tape and its little hand-drawn arrow. His face did some‐
thing very small and very bright, like a man seeing the first star. “And
this is the family object.”




